# Normalize all text files to LF in the repo. Files already committed with
# CRLF are left alone until an explicit `git add --renormalize .`.
* text=auto

# Windows-native scripts: pin CRLF in the working tree. cmd.exe mis-parses
# LF-only batch files (goto/label resolution), and CRLF is the safe default
# for scripts that legacy PowerShell hosts or double-click execution touch.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# POSIX shell scripts: pin LF. A bash script that picks up CRLF fails with
# cryptic "\r: command not found" errors.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Extensionless bash scripts, pinned BY NAME because the *.sh rule above
# cannot see them. Both are run directly, so a CRLF checkout breaks them the
# same way.
#
# The pin is load-bearing, not belt-and-braces, and the mechanism is not the
# obvious one: `* text=auto` marks these files text, and git checks out a text
# file using core.eol precisely WHEN core.autocrlf is false -- which is the
# setting on this machine. core.eol is unset, so it defaults to `native`, i.e.
# CRLF on Windows. They are LF in the working tree today only because nothing
# has re-checked them out since; `git diff` already warns "LF will be replaced
# by CRLF the next time Git touches it" for every unpinned text file here.
# Verified 2026-09-01.
#
# There is no glob for "file with a shebang": a new extensionless script needs
# its own line here.
scripts/instructions-log text eol=lf
scripts/bootstrap-pre-commit text eol=lf
